checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r8c-editor.md:11:The supported story is that the G7 set a 100-million-barrel release timetable on October 2, “taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled,” with a “frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days,” refinery coordination and a pledge to “refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries.” The [joint statement](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/data/sources/fuel-reserves/govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02.txt>) supplies those terms; the [IEA account](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/data/sources/fuel-reserves/iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02.txt>) assigns “around 325 million barrels” already released to the **March** action.
checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r8c-editor.md:13:The page has the right skeptical angle and states the main unknowns. Its opening presents the October release before explaining the unfinished March action; refinery coordination stays out of the story view. The March tally, pressure on Europe, October decision and export-ban outcome appear in separate paragraphs and a later timeline. No passage near the top gives that sequence with its dates. This previously identified story defect remains visible; I am not filing it again as a new line edit.
