checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r8c-editor.md:48:For the language complaints, in order: **act** on the document-list subject of the unknowns, as item 3; **leave** the pre-decision price dates, but keep them once; **cut** the table-construction caption; **cut** the bare “Source history” citation line; **cut** the redundant March 19 timeline row; **cut** repeated March-overlap wording after its first explanation; **keep one** version each of the Trump and Bessent remarks; **move or remove** the loose Bessent, German, stock and EU lines; **cut** the second telling of October’s four-month figure in the March paragraph. These are decisions on repeated stalls, not newly filed findings.
checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r8c-editor.md:50:For the stranger’s proposed addition, **leave out** the roughly 75-million-barrel subtraction. It is the arithmetic remainder of March’s pledge after the IEA’s 325-million tally, not an allocation of October’s 100 million. For the proposed cut, **cut** the bare “Source history” line; it gives the reader no further decision or proof.
checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r8c-editor.md:54:The headline survives the easiest wrong reading: it never calls all 100 million barrels diesel, and the dek says the diesel amount has no number. The first sentence says what happened and spells out the International Energy Agency. It names no person before introduction. Its weak point is “commitments that have already been fulfilled,” whose March meaning arrives later. The cold reader gets the announcement and uncertainty, but not the short sequence or the refinery part of the decision.
checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r8c-editor.md:60:1. **Major · new · FIX.** Page bytes: “The joint statement takes into account commitments already fulfilled, with the release to begin immediately, and calls the diesel share substantial in the first 20 days.” Reason: *share* suggests a stated portion of the 100 million; the statement characterizes the release, not a calculated share. The [G7 statement](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/data/sources/fuel-reserves/govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02.txt>) says “including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days by G7 members and partners”.
checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r8c-editor.md:62:2. **Minor · new · TIGHTEN.** Page bytes: “The G7 says it will meet again through the International Energy Agency in the coming days to discuss more diesel releases if needed, and it asks the agency for a follow-up report within 20 days on how the measures are working. Separately, the U.S. Energy Department is offering up to 40 million barrels of crude from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve as an exchange, in which companies take oil now and return it later with extra barrels. Bids are due October 6, and deliveries under awarded exchanges are scheduled for November and December.” Reason: one paragraph joins the G7’s future review to a pre-existing US solicitation and makes the reader untangle which deliveries concern earlier awards.
checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r8c-editor.md:64:3. **Major · new · FIX.** Page bytes: “Four things are not in the G7 statement, the French readout, the International Energy Agency's account or the Energy Department's request: how much of the 100 million barrels is beyond the March pledge, how it divides between diesel and crude, which countries will supply it, and whether any of it has been delivered.” Reason: the Energy Department request predates the G7 decision and concerns execution of March’s US share; listing it as a record expected to allocate October gives it the wrong subject. The [request](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/data/sources/fuel-reserves/doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29.txt>) says “continuing DOE’s execution of the 172-million-barrel release previously announced by President Trump in coordination with the International Energy Agency (IEA) member nations’ overall 400-million-barrel commitment.”
checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r8c-editor.md:68:- **What else did the G7 decide about diesel supply?** The [joint statement](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/data/sources/fuel-reserves/govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02.txt>) says, “We will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns and temporarily increase utilization rates where feasible.” This changes answers **1 and 4**. Put that decision in story view in place of the repeated October-volume clause in the March paragraph. This material omission was identified earlier and is not a new numbered finding.
checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r8c-editor.md:70:- **What does the decision aim to change for a diesel user?** The [G7 statement](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/data/sources/fuel-reserves/govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02.txt>) says the measures aim to “shield households and businesses from price shocks”; [Bessent’s post](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/data/sources/fuel-reserves/bessent-post-2026-10-01.txt>) names “American farmers, truckers, and businesses”. This changes answer **2** and displaces the overfull war-and-superlative sentence from the first-read stall. Neither record predicts a particular pump-price change.
checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r8c-editor.md:71:- **How much is new, how much is diesel, who supplies it, and has any October barrel been delivered?** These remain unknown in the checked record, and the page states them as unknown. The [joint statement](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/data/sources/fuel-reserves/govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02.txt>) says “taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled” and promises a “frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days by G7 members and partners”; those words do not supply the missing allocation or receipt. This governs answers **1, 3, 5 and 6**. No extra number belongs in the page.
checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r9-records.md:1:1. **Minor · new · Row 3, what-happened.** Sentence: “The Independent says prices have rocketed in recent months because of the US-Israeli conflict with Iran and Russia's war in Ukraine.” The [pinned IEA account](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/data/sources/fuel-reserves/iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02.txt:7) already connects the disrupted diesel market and higher prices: “with Ukrainian attacks on Russian refineries exacerbating the situation for diesel. This is resulting in tighter markets and higher prices”. The sentence accurately attributes the wording to *The Independent*, but credits an outlet for a causal account available in a pinned primary. The IEA can carry the market explanation; the outlet attribution is needed only for the outlet’s particular wording.
checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r9-records.md:3:2. **Minor · new · Row 15, where-this-sits.** Sentences: “Diesel is 2.485 dollars a gallon, or 63.8 percent, above March 2. Brent is 42.64 dollars a barrel above February 27. WTI is 29.20 dollars above February 27.” The pins give the inputs: [diesel](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/data/sources/fuel-reserves/GASDESW-2026-10-03.csv) has “2026-09-28,6.382” and “2026-03-02,3.897”; [Brent](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/data/sources/fuel-reserves/DCOILBRENTEU-2026-10-03.csv) has “2026-09-29,113.96” and “2026-02-27,71.32”; [WTI](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/data/sources/fuel-reserves/DCOILWTICO-2026-10-03.csv) has “2026-09-29,96.16” and “2026-02-27,66.96”. The stated changes require arithmetic the page does not show. The calculations are correct: 6.382 − 3.897 = 2.485, or 63.8 percent of the March value; the Brent and WTI differences are 42.64 and 29.20.
checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r7-redteam.md:1:No reportable new or residual finding. I checked the built page against all 46 pinned records, the reader model, prior reports, the ledger, and the held list. A search of later primary publications through October 3 found no verified record that makes a page sentence false. The page’s central distinction remains supported: the [G7 statement](https://www.gov.uk/government/news/g7-leaders-statement-on-global-energy-security-and-market-stability) sets the 100 million barrel timetable without a diesel amount, while the [IEA account](https://www.iea.org/news/executive-director-participates-in-g7-leaders-meeting-on-energy-security-and-markets) assigns its 325 million released figure to the March action.
